<div class="tabs"><div class="tabs__bar" role="tablist"><button class="tabs__tab tabs__tab--active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-0" id="tabs-0-tab-0">▸ 1 &nbsp;—&nbsp; The Paper Cutter</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-1" id="tabs-0-tab-1">▸ 2 &nbsp;—&nbsp; In-Itself and For-Itself</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-2" id="tabs-0-tab-2">▸ 3 &nbsp;—&nbsp; What Radical Freedom Means</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-3" id="tabs-0-tab-3">▸ 4 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Responsibility and Choosing for All</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-4" id="tabs-0-tab-4">▸ 5 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Reading Guide: The First Half of the Lecture</button></div><div class="tabs__panel tabs__panel--active" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-0" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-0"><div>
<p>Sartre opens with a thought experiment that is deceptively simple. Consider a paper cutter — a small blade for opening envelopes or cutting paper. Before the artisan who made it picked up the metal, they had a concept of what they were making: what the object was <em>for</em>, what shape it needed to be, how it would work. The design, the function, the purpose — all of this existed in the mind of the maker before the object existed in the world.</p>
<p>In philosophical terms: the <em>essence</em> (what it is, what it’s for) preceded the <em>existence</em> (the fact that there is one). The made object has a nature that was fixed before it existed. That nature is not its fault, so to speak — it was imposed from outside, by a mind that designed it with a purpose.</p>
<p>Now consider the traditional religious picture of human beings. If God created you — if there is a divine artisan who had in mind what a human being is supposed to be, what function we are to serve, what kind of creatures we are — then the same structure applies. Essence (your nature, your purpose, your place in the divine plan) precedes existence (the particular life you are living). On this view, to live well is to live in accordance with a nature that was given to you, that you did not choose.</p>
<p>Sartre thinks this picture was abandoned by most educated people long before the twentieth century. But he notices something: even atheists often kept the structure while dropping the deity. Eighteenth-century atheists, he argues, eliminated God but retained the idea of a fixed human nature — a universal human essence defined by reason, or feeling, or sociality, or whatever else. They kept essence-before-existence without the theism that originally justified it.</p>
<p>Sartre’s move is to reject the structure entirely, not just the deity. If there is no God, then there is no divine artisan who designed you. And if there is no designer, there is no design. Which means: you exist first, and whatever essence you have — whatever you become — is something you make, not something you were given. <strong>Existence precedes essence.</strong></p>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-1" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-1" hidden=""><div>
<h3>Two kinds of being</h3>
<p>To understand why existence precedes essence for human beings, you need Sartre’s most fundamental distinction, which comes from <em>Being and Nothingness</em> and underpins everything in the lecture.</p>
<p>Sartre distinguishes two fundamental modes of being:</p>
<div>
